# Normalise line endings in the repository to LF, whatever the contributor's
# core.autocrlf happens to be.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true (the Git for Windows
# default) turns every file into CRLF, which is wrong for the sibling .cmd and
# .ps1 launchers in particular: cmd.exe and powershell.exe want either LF or
# CRLF consistently, and mixed endings are the combination that actually breaks
# them. `eol=lf` here overrides autocrlf, so every clone gets byte-identical
# files and the diffs stay clean.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never touch these, and keep GitHub from guessing.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.wav binary

# Windows launchers read in the console code page, so they are deliberately
# ASCII-only; declaring them text keeps the LF normalisation above.
*.cmd text
*.ps1 text
